Networks built with random weights serve as the origin point for understanding how information propagates across layers and how specific subsets of neurons and connections can be trained to replicate the capabilities of their dense, pre-trained counterparts. This survey of research on winning lottery tickets offers a comprehensive overview of the field and examines the implications of these findings for network design. Specifically, it highlights that convolutional architectures contain sparser subnetworks with localized connectivity capable of matching the performance of dense networks with random weights. This supports the conclusion that specialized, localized subnetworks are the winning lottery tickets within convolutional networks. Furthermore, this perspective suggests that future efforts should focus on identifying these efficient subnetworks to guide the development of more effective models. Additionally, the article explores related concepts such as structured pruning and structured training, which also utilize sparse subnetworks to achieve results comparable to dense networks. Ultimately, these approaches find their foundation in the principles of winning lottery tickets. The paper concludes by outlining potential future research directions, inspired by the insights gained from lottery tickets. Specifically, there is a need to investigate the origins of these efficient subnetworks within randomly initialized models and to understand how their localized structure can inform the development of even more specialized and performant models. By addressing these questions, the field can continue to advance the efficiency and capabilities of artificial neural networks. \end{abstract}
