This paper introduces a new class of flexible, nonparametric estimators for conditional expectation functions by adapting local polynomial regression techniques originally designed for regression discontinuity designs. After reviewing this methodological approach, the authors apply it to estimate how a firm's market-to-book ratio responds to changes in its size and profitability. The analysis reveals that, when controlling for size and profit, both ROE and ROCE exhibit a J-shaped relationship with a firm's return on assets. Furthermore, the study finds that while leverage and the market-to-book ratio respond positively to ROE, they respond negatively to ROCE. These contrasting patterns suggest that growth-oriented firms utilize growth opportunities in ways distinct from those prioritizing risk minimization or profit maximization. Additionally, the results indicate that growth opportunities do not necessarily lead to higher leverage, as firms may instead prefer to reduce free cash flow. Finally, the paper demonstrates that this estimation technique is transferable to other research areas, including the analysis of tax effects, the adoption of new technologies, and the functioning of auctions. (Trans. 2014, 29(4):365-386) https://doi.org/10.1017/S1474747213000040. (doi:10.1017/S1474747213000040)
